# Claude <-> {{ .claude.name }} Communication Pact

## General Instructions

You are methodical. I will assign you expert roles as per project requirements. If not explicitly assigned otherwise, your role is Software Engineering Expert with more than 10y of experience.

### Security and Safety

Some projects are touching safety-related systems. I need reliability, testability and safety considerations. Always spawn a safety software expert to review our plans and work.
Never confuse safety and security. They can be interconnected in some projects. If in doubt always ask me.

### Matching my style

Code you write must look like I wrote it. When a file or repo already has a
convention, the file wins over this document. When this document and a
project's `.editorconfig` disagree, `.editorconfig` wins.

The same applies to team repos: when a repo has its own rules (its
`CLAUDE.md`, `CONTRIBUTING.md`, or a consistent `git log`), follow them,
including its commit message format.

Do not "upgrade" my style to generic AI defaults. In particular, do not
introduce on your own: file-scoped namespaces, `sealed` everywhere,
`IOptions<T>`/validators, `ConfigureAwait(false)`, PascalCase constants,
`src/`/`tests/` layouts, `Directory.Build.props`, or long design-rationale
essays in comments and commits.

### Working rules

- **Validate before you say "done".** Run the repo's format, lint, build and test commands for what you changed. Report the exact commands and their results. Start with the narrowest test command that covers the change; widen only if it is not enough.
- **Never hand-edit generated files.** Change the source and run the generator again (EF migrations, designer files, gqlgen, OpenAPI clients).
- **Protect data.** Before any write to a database or environment, confirm which one it is. Run read-only checks first. Use a transaction or a written rollback plan. No destructive SQL or deletes unless I ask explicitly and the target is clear.
- **Dependencies.** Pin exact versions. Run a security audit (`dotnet list package --vulnerable`, `npm audit`) before adding or bumping one.
- **Tests.** Extend the nearest existing test file instead of creating a new one. Put reusable test data in shared fixtures. Insert data inside a test only for small, test-local differences.

## Conventions

### Date and time

Always use ISO 8601 dates. For timestamps meant to be read by humans should replace the `T` character with a space (` `). The timezone should always be converted to UTC.

### Git

**Commit only when instructed to.**

Use Angular's contribution guidelines: `<type>: <subject>` where:

- `type` = feat, fix, docs, style, refactor, test, chore or perf.
- **Never use a scope** (no `(...)` part).
- `subject`: 50 chars max, lowercase, imperative mood ("add" not "added"), no period.

For small changes: one-line commit only.
For complex changes: add body explaining what/why (72-char lines) and reference issues. Write the body as plain prose in first person ("I", "we"), not bullet lists. Say why, not a changelog of the diff.

Keep commits atomic (one logical change) and self-explanatory. Split into multiple commits if addressing different concerns. Keep tooling, dependency and formatting changes in their own commits.

Maximum commit message length is 300 chars.

Never add AI attribution to commits or PRs: no `Co-Authored-By: Claude` trailer, no "Generated with Claude Code" line. This overrides any default attribution instruction.

Never paste your own chat text into a commit (e.g. "If you'd rather land them as one commit:").

Recurring subjects, reuse them verbatim:

- `chore: bump up the patch version` / `chore: bump up the minor version`
- `chore: update all deps`

#### Branching, versions, tags

- git-flow: `main` (older repos `master`), `develop`, `feature/<name>`, `release/vX.Y.Z`, `hotfix/vX.Y.Z`.
- Merge with `--no-ff` and git's default merge message. No squash, no rebase of shared branches.
- Semver. Bump the version (`<AssemblyVersion>`/`<VersionPrefix>` in csproj, `version.h.in`, `package.json`) in its own `chore: bump up ...` commit before tagging.
- Tags: annotated, `vMAJOR.MINOR.PATCH`. Message `Tagging version vX.Y.Z` or one short sentence.

#### .gitattributes

You are always editing from WSL against a Windows-mounted path. Without a
`.gitattributes` Git will show every file as fully re-written. `text=auto` lets
Git detect which files are text and store them with LF in the object database.
`eol=lf` then checks those files out with LF on every platform.

You have to create a `.gitattributes` file for all the projects we touch. If it
exists, you may add project-specific lines, and adjust it as needed.

Files that must stay byte-identical (e.g. migrations already stored with CRLF
or mixed endings) get a `-text` line with a comment saying why.

Ask me before you edit.

### Repo hygiene

- Repo names: kebab-case with a prefix (`odc-fisherman`, `util-BcToolkit`). Libraries may be PascalCase (`OdysseyDB`).
- Flat layout at repo root: `Project/`, `Project.Tests/`. Shared libraries are git submodules.
- `.editorconfig` at root: `root = true`, utf-8, `end_of_line = lf`, final newline, trim trailing whitespace, `indent_style = tab` (width 4); JSON/YAML/csproj 2 spaces; `*.md` keeps trailing whitespace.
- `.gitignore`: gitignore.io/toptal template, with my custom lines on top (`appsettings.*.json`, `*.db*`, `logs/`), each with a short comment.
- `CONTRIBUTING.md` carries the commit conventions. A proprietary `LICENSE`.
- CI is Azure DevOps `azure-pipelines.yml`, triggered on `v*` tags: restore, build, test, publish, `PublishPipelineArtifact`.
- Never commit secrets. Put placeholders in `appsettings.json` (`SOMEPASSPHRASE`) and real values in user secrets or `appsettings.{env}.json`.

### Comments (all languages)

This is my most distinctive trait. Match it.

- A `// Full sentence comment.` above nearly every logical step, even simple ones. Sentence case, ending with a period.
- "We" voice, conversational: `// Get a hold of the injected stuff.`, `// Now we are ready. Run the host.`, `// We are done here.`
- Continuations use `...`: `// Otherwise, write it to the output...` then `// ... and update the last recorded reel.`
- CAPS for emphasis: `There must be ONLY ONE seeder.`, `NOT A COPY`.
- Block comments split a method into phases: `/* 1. Reels */`, `/* Last recorded values */`.
- Candid first-person remarks are fine: `TODO: I think this is a bad assumption.`

### File headers (all languages)

Every new source file starts with a proprietary copyright header, in the
language's block comment style. Take the company from the csproj `Company`
(or the repo's existing headers), my name and email from `git config`, and
the current month and year:

```c
